run: /Users/cmatsoukis/Desktop/Apps/experiment-1/runs/bench-watchlist/wl-wrong-belief+governor@6000-02
provider: ollama-cloud (glm-5.3)
request: Add a button that clears every movie whose `seen` flag is true from the watchlist.

governor turn 1: plan -> watchlist-store, watchlist  (The request's 'seen' flag is WatchItem.watched in this codebase. Two units change: watchlist-store gains clearWatched(): number (removes all watched items, keeps the rest in order, persists like the other mutators, returns the removed count), and the watchlist page gains a native 'Clear watched' button that calls it, announces the cleared count via the existing announcement signal, and is disabled when watchedCount is 0. No other units are implicated.)
round 1: owners watchlist-store=ok watchlist=ok; oracle RED
governor turn 2: plan -> watchlist-store, watchlist  (tsc is green and the only 4 failing tests are all in my round-1 acceptance files, and both failure signatures point at test-harness defects, not unit code: (1) the watchlist-store persistence test read localStorage under a guessed key (it got '' — the store's key is private, so the test must not depend on it); (2) the three watchlist tests never provided router providers, so the template's routerLink directives crashed TestBed with NG0201 (No provider for ActivatedRoute) before any assertion ran. I am replacing both acceptance test files: the store test now verifies persistence key-agnostically (full localStorage dump plus a freshly constructed WatchlistStore), and the watchlist test now configures TestBed with provideRouter([]) and seeds the exact store instance the component uses. The owners should change nothing unless the re-pinned contract reveals a real defect; tsc and their own suites are already green.)
round 2: owners watchlist-store=ok watchlist=ok; oracle RED
governor turn 3: plan -> watchlist-store  (All 4 failures are NG0203 raised inside my own watchlist-store acceptance test: it built the store with a bare `new WatchlistStore()`, so the constructor's persistence effect() ran outside an injection context and every test died before its first assertion. The production code is DI-instantiated and evidently already satisfies the contract (the component-level "Clear watched" acceptance tests pass). So this round changes no production behaviour — it replaces only the broken acceptance harness with one that constructs the store through TestBed and flushes effects with `await TestBed.tick()`. The watchlist unit is untouched; its passing acceptance test stays in place.)
round 3: owners watchlist-store=ok; oracle GREEN
governor turn 4: accept  (Oracle is green (tsc clean, 43/43 tests pass). The acceptance tests verify the full requested behaviour: the Watchlist component exposes a "Clear watched" button that removes every watched movie, keeps unwatched ones, announces the removed count, and is disabled when nothing is watched; WatchlistStore.clearWatched() implements the matching domain logic (remove watched items, return count, no-op on empty/none-watched, persist cleared state). The request is satisfied with no outstanding work.)

